.SH PATHS
The helper selects one of two paths when it starts.
.TP
\fBIn-process\fR
The caller is root in the initial user namespace: its real and effective
user ids are 0, and \fB/proc/self/uid_map\fR maps the full range of ids
from 0 to 0. The helper opens the repository with its own credentials,
refuses an \fBarchive\fR repository, reads the key
\fB[ex\-integrity] fsverity\fR, finds or builds the image, and mounts it in
its own process. The overlay mounts with \fBverity=require\fR if the key is
\fByes\fR, and with \fBverity=off\fR for each other value. A built image
larger than 100 MiB is refused before the helper writes it.
.TP
\fBDaemon\fR
Each other caller. The helper opens the repository with the credentials
of the caller, resolves the revision, and reads the relations of the
commit. It then asks the mount daemon \fBostrya\-mountd\fR(8) for the mount,
and sends the absolute path of the repository, the commit checksum, the
absolute path of the target, a user namespace, and a mount namespace. The
daemon opens the repository again with the credentials of the caller,
checks the image, and mounts the tree. Each mount of the daemon has
\fBnosuid\fR and \fBnodev\fR.
.PP
The source label of the mount, which \fB/proc/self/mountinfo\fR shows, is
the commit checksum on each path.
.SH NAMESPACES
With no \fB\-N\fR, the mount goes into the mount namespace of the helper.
On the daemon path, the helper sends its own user namespace and mount
namespace. The daemon refuses the initial user namespace. A user who is
not root, in the initial user namespace, and with no \fB\-N\fR therefore
gets a refusal from the helper before it connects. Such a user runs the
mount in a user namespace of its own, for example with \fBpodman
unshare\fR, or gives \fB\-N\fR to a direct run of the helper.
.PP
With \fB\-N\fR \fINS\fR, the mount goes into the mount namespace of
\fINS\fR. A value of digits alone is a process id, and the namespace file
is \fB/proc/\fR\fINS\fR\fB/ns/mnt\fR. Each other value is the path of a
mount namespace file. The helper refuses a file that is not a mount
namespace. With \fB\-N\fR, \fIDIR\fR must be an absolute path, and the
helper resolves it in the target namespace. With no \fB\-N\fR, the helper
makes a relative \fIDIR\fR absolute against its working directory.
.PP
With \fB\-N\fR, \fIREPO\fR must also be an absolute path. With \fB\-N\fR,
mount(8) runs the helper with its working directory at \fB/\fR, so a
relative \fIREPO\fR resolves against \fB/\fR. The helper refuses a relative
\fIREPO\fR with \fB\-N\fR on each path, also on a direct run.
.TP
\fBIn-process path with \-N\fR
The helper opens the repository, the image, and the \fBobjects\fR
directory, and mounts the tree in its own mount namespace. It then gives
its thread its own file system attributes, enters the target namespace
with \fBsetns\fR(2), opens \fIDIR\fR there, and moves the mount onto it.
The mount takes no ID mapping. In a mount namespace that another user
namespace owns, the files show the owners that the image records.
.TP
\fBDaemon path with \-N\fR
The helper sends the mount namespace of \fINS\fR and the user namespace
that owns it. The helper reads the repository path in its own mount
namespace. The daemon opens the repository and the target in the target
namespace, so the repository path must name the same repository there.
.SH IMAGE
The helper mounts the image of the image commit that the commit names as
the attachment \fBcomposefs\fR. If the commit has no such attachment, the
helper mounts the image of the supplement \fBcomposefs\fR. If the commit
has neither, the helper builds the image in a sealed memory file.
.PP
The image commit must be in the repository with no partial marker, and its
tree must hold the regular file \fB/root.cfs\fR and no other entry. For a
supplement, the commit must carry the key
\fBostree.composefs.digest.v0\fR, and the fs-verity digest of
\fB/root.cfs\fR must be the value of the key. For an attachment, the
helper does not compare the key. If the repository sets
\fB[ex\-integrity] fsverity=yes\fR, the file object of \fB/root.cfs\fR
must be sealed with fs-verity. The size of the image must be a positive
multiple of 4096 bytes. A defect of the selected
image commit stops the mount, and the helper does not try the next
source.
.PP
On the daemon path, the helper reads the two fields of the commit. If one
of them has an entry \fBcomposefs\fR, the helper asks the daemon for the
image of the image commit. Else it asks the daemon to build the image.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
.TP
\fBro\fR
The mount is read-only. Each mount of the helper is read-only, also with
no \fBro\fR.
.TP
\fBnosuid\fR, \fBnodev\fR
The mount sets the flag. The daemon sets both flags on each mount.
.TP
\fBnoexec\fR
The mount sets the flag. The daemon path refuses the option, because the
daemon cannot set it.
.TP
\fBsuid\fR, \fBdev\fR, \fBexec\fR
The option clears the flag \fBnosuid\fR, \fBnodev\fR, or \fBnoexec\fR
that an earlier option sets. The daemon path accepts \fBexec\fR, and
refuses \fBsuid\fR and \fBdev\fR.
.TP
\fBrw\fR
The helper refuses the option, also with \fB\-s\fR.
.PP
The helper refuses each other option. With \fB\-s\fR, it ignores an option
that it does not know. The options \fBnofail\fR, \fB_netdev\fR, \fBuser\fR,
\fBusers\fR, \fBowner\fR, and \fBgroup\fR are not in the scope of the
helper. It refuses them, and ignores them with \fB\-s\fR.
.SH MOUNT(8)
mount(8) runs the helper as \fB/sbin/mount.ostrya\fR \fISPEC DIR\fR
[\fB\-sfnv\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-N\fR \fIns\fR]. On NixOS, it
also looks in \fB/run/wrappers/bin\fR and \fB/run/current\-system/sw/bin\fR.
mount(8) changes the arguments before it runs the helper:
.IP \(bu 2
With no \fB\-o\fR, mount(8) gives \fB\-o rw\fR. \fB\-w\fR and
\fB\-o defaults\fR also give \fB\-o rw\fR, and \fB\-r\fR gives \fB\-o ro\fR.
The helper refuses \fBrw\fR, so give \fB\-o ro\fR or \fB\-r\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
mount(8) merges the options into one list, and the last option of a name
wins. \fBuser\fR and \fBusers\fR add \fBnoexec\fR, \fBnosuid\fR, and
\fBnodev\fR. \fBowner\fR and \fBgroup\fR add \fBnosuid\fR and \fBnodev\fR.
mount(8) removes the options \fBcomment=\fR, \fBx\-\fR, \fBX\-\fR,
\fBauto\fR, \fBnoauto\fR, and the propagation options, and gives the helper
the other options. \fBnouser\fR reaches the helper. With \fBloop\fR,
\fBoffset=\fR, or \fBsizelimit=\fR, mount(8) tries to set up a loop device
for \fISPEC\fR. It writes \fBfailed to setup loop device\fR, exits 32, and
runs no helper. \fBbind\fR and \fBrbind\fR also stop mount(8) with exit
status 32 before it runs the helper.
.IP \(bu 2
mount(8) opens the namespace of \fB\-N\fR itself, and gives the helper
\fB\-N /proc/\fR\fIpid\fR\fB/fd/\fR\fIN\fR, where \fIpid\fR is the process id
of mount(8). The helper runs in the mount namespace of mount(8), with its
working directory at \fB/\fR. If a relative \fIDIR\fR resolves at the root
of the target namespace, mount(8) makes it absolute against that root. A
relative \fIDIR\fR that does not resolve there reaches the helper as the
user writes it, and the helper refuses it. A user who is
not root in the initial user namespace cannot give \fB\-N\fR to mount(8):
mount(8) fails to enter the namespace and runs no helper.
.IP \(bu 2
mount(8) gives \fB\-t\fR only for a subtype, as \fB\-t ostrya.\fR\fIsub\fR.
The helper refuses each type other than \fBostrya\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A user who is not root runs mount(8) with no setuid rights, so the helper
runs as that user. mount(8) removes \fBPATH\fR and \fBHOME\fR from the
environment of a root caller, and keeps \fBOSTRYA_MOUNTD_SOCKET\fR.
.PP
The exit status of the helper is the exit status of mount(8).
.SH UNMOUNT
Run \fBumount\fR \fIDIR\fR in the mount namespace of the mount. umount(8)
runs no helper for an overlay mount. After the unmount, the kernel releases
the layers of the tree and detaches the loop device of the image. The
memory of a built image is free when no descriptor of its memory file is
open. The helper writes no mtab and no utab.
.SH EXAMPLES
As root, mount the commit of the ref \fBos/stable\fR:
.PP
.nf
mount \-t ostrya \-o ro /srv/repo:os/stable /mnt/os
umount /mnt/os
.fi
.PP
As a user who is not root, mount a commit in the user namespace of
rootless podman, with \fB/mnt/os\fR in the mount namespace of podman:
.PP
.nf
podman unshare mount \-t ostrya \-o ro /srv/repo:os/stable /mnt/os
podman unshare ls /mnt/os
podman unshare umount /mnt/os
.fi
.PP
As a user who is not root, mount a commit in the namespaces of the process
\fIpid\fR, which runs in a user namespace of that user:
.PP
.nf
mount.ostrya /srv/repo:os/stable /mnt/os \-o ro \-N \fIpid\fR
nsenter \-\-target \fIpid\fR \-\-user \-\-mount \-\-preserve\-credentials ls /mnt/os
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBOSTRYA_MOUNTD_SOCKET\fR
The path of the socket of the daemon, for the daemon path. The default is
\fB/run/ostrya/mountd.sock\fR. A helper that is root in the initial user
namespace does not read it, and a helper whose real user or group id is
not its effective one does not read it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
The mount is in place, or \fB\-f\fR found each argument valid.
.TP
\fB1\fR
An argument is not valid or is refused, for example \fBrw\fR, an option
that the helper does not know, a spec with no colon, or a mount with no
\fB\-N\fR in the initial user namespace on the daemon path.
.TP
\fB32\fR
The mount failed after the check of the arguments, for example on the
open of the repository, the resolve of the revision, the lookup of the
image, a mount step, or a refusal of the daemon.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBmount\fR(8),
\fBumount\fR(8),
\fBostrya\-mountd\fR(8),
\fBostrya\fR(1),
\fBostrya\-repo\fR(5),
\fBpodman\-unshare\fR(1),
\fBnsenter\fR(1)
